TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit provides approximately constant local solar time at a given latitude; it does not guarantee continuous sunlight. The report's claims of a constant sunlight supply and that the descending path is constantly in sunlight are not generally valid."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The reported RAAN value does not indicate the launch facility location; RAAN specifies the orientation of the orbital plane relative to the reference direction. Launch-site access depends on launch azimuth, launch window, and achievable inclination."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says the orbit is circular and that argument of perigee and true anomaly are undefined, but then lists argument of latitude as 0° without specifying an epoch or initial state. A complete initial orbit state needs a defined epoch and a consistent set of elements."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The proposed Vega E launch is presented as a likely 2025 option, although its development and schedule were uncertain; the report itself notes that the vehicle had not been built. A rideshare to a 600 km, 97.79° orbit is not demonstrated to be available."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table includes apparent duplicate access entries and inconsistent date/time ordering, so the stated frequency and mean contact duration should be checked against the STK scenario and report interval."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The claimed high pass frequency from a more polar ground station is not a sufficient basis for selecting a site. Access depends on station latitude, orbit geometry, elevation mask, and the chosen analysis period; no elevation mask or link budget is given."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail can increase atmospheric drag, but the report gives no sail area, mass, ballistic coefficient, deployment reliability, or orbit-decay estimate to show that the spacecraft will deorbit within an acceptable timeframe."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report identifies 600 km altitude and orbital inclination but does not substantiate the imaging design with ground sample distance, sensor performance, swath, or revisit analysis; the stated whole-reef daily coverage is not demonstrated."}],"overall":"The submission presents a coherent preliminary mission concept and meaningful STK-based orbit and access work. It addresses most required topics, but several design claims are unsupported or technically imprecise, the launch option is speculative, and the EOL and imaging performance are not quantitatively validated. Supporting software files and stronger citation and figure conventions are needed.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the Australian reef-monitoring need, primary imaging mission, and rationale for one spacecraft. It identifies likely government and science users, but does not clearly distinguish the mission operator from customers or specify a definite operator. The rationale and user descriptions are useful but not fully detailed or consistently supported by references.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The spacecraft is intended to image and send data to ground stations for collation and analysis."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit elements, STK access analysis, a five-year duration, and a passive drag-sail EOL concept, with useful screenshots and tabulated results. However, the launch choice is speculative, mission performance is not tied to sensor resolution or data needs, and the STK access output contains apparent inconsistencies. Some orbital and sunlight claims are inaccurate, and EOL implementation is not substantiated with calculations or software files.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the selected launch, orbit, and ground station and acknowledges launch uncertainty. It reaches relevant but mostly generic conclusions and does not critically assess whether the design meets imaging, revisit, or data-delivery needs.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has the expected sections and a broadly logical structure, and includes useful visual and tabular material. Presentation is weakened by grammar and typographical issues, inconsistent or absent figure/table numbering and titles, awkwardly rendered tables, and incomplete web-reference formatting. Wikipedia is used as a source despite the task's restriction.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."}]}]}
